ed  goffertson carries a new weight of the  legacy left by the very institution that  made his job necessary it was  a quick decision even though it wasn't a  light one  to have the kids return back to their  communities more than 200 unmarked  burials found here are set to be exhumed  for identification and potentially  reunited with the communities they were  taken from it's sacred ground and it's  of the utmost importance to leave them  where they are  but  as we can all  admit and know  this isn't your typical situation  monday's memorial marked one year since  the remains were detected at a nearby  old apple orchard on the site of the  former school grounds  ultimately  we have a fundamental human right  to bury our loved ones at home  some are firmly against that plan and  gotfordson whose mother was sent to the  institution serves on a committee to  determine the best way forward we all  understand  what it means to move the kids to  disturb them  there's no one who doesn't  understand that the process includes  oral tellings from elders who survived  guiding more ground penetrating radar  work and sharing memories of loved ones  who went missing  these are steps we have to take before  we even  get the arc the forensic archaeologists  in there to begin their work because  once they start  we're we're heading for a world of hurt  for our people in terms of a timeline of  where are things at now and how long  could it take  years  it's going to take years  and a lot of money which he says the  federal government should provide the  prime minister only making a broad  promise during his visit here this week  whatever communities decide is the right  path for them the federal government  will be there to support  so many extremely sensitive and  difficult decisions are being made here  and it's an uncharted path so goffertson  says they're taking their time while  prioritizing science and cultural  protocols chris  nitu garcia in kamloops bc tonight thank  you neethu and a reminder there is  support available for survivors of  residential schools and their families  the number is toll free it operates 24  hours a day it's 1-800-721-0066  up next firing back 10 years after  america's deadliest school shooting hope  this is the turning point  i just worry that we are becoming numb  to this that we are accepting this as  the new normal this is the only place in  the world that this happens there's not  a single country in the world in which  kids fear for their lives when they walk  into school in the morning  that's us senator chris murphy echoing  what many people americans and otherwise  feel in the wake of the senseless  massacre in texas many people point an  accusing finger at the gun lobby after  tragedies like this continue to happen  this weekend the national rifle  association gathers in texas but the  organization has a long history of  deflecting criticism of gun violence  away from gun ownership and  accessibility eric sorenson looks at the  uphill battle to change gun laws in  america  uvalde texas is like all mass shootings  from virginia tech to parkland florida  they are tragic and senseless but it is  most like sandy hook in newtown  connecticut young children slaughtered  at school without a clue what was  happening or why but there is a  disheartening difference after sandy  hook there was a glimmer of hope for  change to gun laws in the u.s i really  really believe this will be the start of  something in 1994 a temporary ban on  assault weapons brought fewer mass  killings in the united states the ban  expired but a spate of shootings  culminating in sandy hook brought new  resolve that something would be done  about guns i'll protect our second  amendment rights conservative democrat  joe manchin said even he was ready to  act i ask all my friends in nra we need  to sit down and move this dialogue to a  sensible reasonable approach but the  national rifle association had its own  solutions to put armed police in every  school the only thing  that stops a bad guy with a gun  is a good guy with a gun and since then  guns and mass shootings have  proliferated and today senator manchin  calls for background checks but not  restricting gun ownership and we have to  reach across the bench but we can't  politicize it has to be done in such a  practical way it just makes sense  nowadays republican senators have a  tried and true response to guns the most  effective tool for keeping kids safe  is armed law enforcement on the campus  we know what is effective and it is  targeting the felons and fugitives and  the bad guys  but never targeting the guns now in the  u.s there are more guns than people and  for those who want change there is only  boiling frustration nowhere else do  little kids go to school thinking that  they might be shot that day nowhere else  does that happen except here in the  united states of america and it is a  choice a choice like the choice today by  texas lawmakers to focus on mental  health not the gunman's assault weapons  so when texas democrat beto o'rourke  stepped forward to call them out he was  shouted down i can't believe you're a  sick son that would come to a deal like  this to make a political issue here when  are we going to do something basketball  coach steve kerr like many americans  knows that life and basketball will just  go on i'm sorry i'm tired of the moments  of silence  enough  right now in the u.s there is anger and  pressure to do more but history suggests  the pressure usually passes and the  anger will fade  erik sorensen global news toronto  and that's global national for this  wednesday i'm chris galas we'll leave  you tonight with images that say more  than words ever could about the grief in  uvalde 21 lives taken 19 of them young  children and yet another brutal mass  shooting in the u.s now all too familiar  for that country with no resolution in  sight  thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

